  "body": "The `contextmenu` event should be considered a user activation because it indicates that the user is interacting with the page.\n\nFirefox already treats `contextmenu` events as user activation, hence allows web apps to use restricted APIs like `navigator.vibrate()`.  In Chromium, we are [on track](https://www.chromestatus.com/feature/5726389672083456) to match the Firefox behavior because on mobile platforms, a web app should be able to mimic the native behavior of vibrating the device on long-press gestures (which fire `contextmenu` events).  Here is a [demo](output.jsbin.com/bonadab).\n\nNote that on desktop, both Chromium and Firefox already treat `contextmenu` events as user activation (e.g. `navigator.vibrate` returns `true` in the above demo).\n\nThis PR is remotely related to #1903 (which is a work in progress in Chromium).\n\n\n<!--\n    This comment and the below content is programatically generated.\n    You may add a comma-separated list of anchors you'd like a\n    direct link to below (e.g. #idl-serializers, #idl-sequence):\n\n    Don't remove this comment or modify anything below this line.\n    If you don't want a preview generated for this pull request,\n    just replace the whole of this comment's content by \"no preview\"\n    and remove what's below.\n-->\n***\n<a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/3659/acknowledgements.html\" title=\"Last updated on May 2, 2018, 2:25 PM GMT (ff41c10)\">/acknowledgements.html</a>  ( <a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/3659/423c664...ff41c10/acknowledgements.html\" title=\"Last updated on May 2, 2018, 2:25 PM GMT (ff41c10)\">diff</a> )\n<a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/3659/interaction.html\" title=\"Last updated on May 2, 2018, 2:25 PM GMT (ff41c10)\">/interaction.html</a>  ( <a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/3659/423c664...ff41c10/interaction.html\" title=\"Last updated on May 2, 2018, 2:25 PM GMT (ff41c10)\">diff</a> )",
